<h2>Indian organic cow manure for export</h2>
<p>Cow manure exports from India serve two quite different buyer types: horticulture and garden-retail programmes in Europe and North America looking for a natural soil-improvement input, and plantation agriculture in the Gulf, Maldives and Southeast Asia where soil organic matter is the limiting factor.</p>

<p>We supply composted, sun-dried and screened material in powder, granule and cake form, sourced from dairy belts in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh, Punjab and Haryana.</p>

<h2>On the word "organic"</h2>
<p>We want to be direct about this, because it's the most common source of confusion on this product. We use "organic manure" and "organic fertilizer" to describe what the product <em>is</em> — an animal-origin, non-synthetic soil input. That is a product-type description. It is not a claim that the product is certified under NPOP, USDA, EU Organic or any other scheme, and we don't make such claims.</p>
<p>If your buyer or your market requires product certified under a specific organic standard, say so at the enquiry stage. We'd rather tell you honestly at the start than have a shipment rejected at your end.</p>

<h2>Check your import rules before ordering</h2>
<p>Animal-origin fertilizers face tighter import controls than most agricultural goods. Many countries require a prior import permit, specific heat-treatment or composting declarations, or a phytosanitary certificate with additional declarations attached. This varies enormously — some markets are straightforward, others effectively closed without a permit.</p>
<p>{{ site.complianceChecklistCta }} Send us your destination country early and we'll confirm what documentation we can provide against your requirements.</p>

<h2>What should I specify?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — powder, granule or cake. This affects handling, spreading and container loading.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — 15–25% is the typical range. Lower moisture means more usable material per tonne shipped.</li>
  <li><strong>Screening mesh</strong> — 2mm, 4mm or your own requirement, to remove stones, straw and debris.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong> — bulk jumbo bags for agricultural use, or retail-format bags with your artwork for garden-centre programmes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian cow manure?</h2>
<p>The available trade data for HS 3101 reports Italy, France and the United States among destination markets, with the Maldives, Singapore, Nepal and Gulf markets (for plantation use) also named in secondary sources. The table below shows what we could verify — and we'd flag that this dataset is lower-confidence than others on this site, which the source note explains rather than glossing over.</p>

<h2>Cow manure export pricing</h2>
<p>Pricing depends mainly on form, moisture specification, screening and packing format — retail-format bagging costs materially more than bulk jumbo bags. Freight is a large share of landed cost on a low-value-density product like this, so destination port matters. <a href="/get-a-quote/">Send your requirement</a> for a current FOB or CIF quote.</p>

